{
"e1": 67,
"e2": 68,
"n": 1,
"html": "<p>I found my dialogue with my subconscious especially amplified with <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" href=\"http://loveyhuasca.info/\">loveyhuasca</a>. My experiences included a much deeper understanding of <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" href=\"http://motivations.u-dont-exist.com/\">my motivations</a>, peaceful loving awareness operating alongside analytical clarity, the disappearance of unhelpful daydreaming, different priorities around relationships, stronger intuition, and repeated coordination between intuition, subconscious response, and conscious analysis.</p>"
},
{
"e1": 250,
"e2": 251,
"n": 1,
"html": "<p>Aside from focusing on things in your direct experience, you can also <a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer nofollow\" href=\"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/00029157.2018.1496318\">suggest yourself into greater alertness</a> (it's still relaxed-alert, not paranoid-alert). Keep your eyes open. Use a small hand movement each time you make the auto-suggestion that you're gaining increasing clarity. Then stop the hand movement and see where your mind is at. That hand movement grounds and aligns the distractible mind.</p>"
},
{
"e1": 528,
"e2": 529,
"n": 1,
"html": "<p>I don't use NLP to convince anyone an intuition is true. For example, I don't use NLP or hypnosis in general, to do more than initial memory retrieval work. Memories retrieved still need external validation, especially when the questions led to them (which NLP tends to do).</p>"
}
]
